<TopicPage slug="subjects/media-studies/foundational/the-theoretical-framework">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Media Studies is not watching television with opinions. The WJEC Eduqas GCSE is built on a
    <strong>theoretical framework</strong> — a fixed set of four questions you can aim at any
    media product, from a 1960s newspaper front page to this morning's TikTok. Every product in
    every one of the nine forms the specification studies (advertising and marketing, film,
    magazines, music video, newspapers, radio, television, video games, and online, social and
    participatory media) is analysed through the same lens. Learn the four areas and you have
    learned the grammar of the entire subject.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why a framework at all? Because media products are <em>constructed</em> — someone chose
    this camera angle, this headline word, this star, this release date — and choices mean
    things. A photograph of a politician shot from below reads as powerful; the same politician
    mid-blink on page three reads as foolish. Neither is "just what happened". The four areas
    are the systematic way of asking who constructed what, how, for whom, and with whose money
    — and the reason they come as a set is that the answers interlock, as the widget shows.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the framework, one area at a time — each area comes with its own guiding question" widget="equationStep" config={fwCfg} />

  <h2>Media language: how it makes its meaning</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Media language</strong> is how the media, through their forms, codes and
    conventions, communicate meanings. <em>Codes</em> are the sign systems a form uses: camera
    shots and angles, lighting, mise-en-scène (everything placed in the frame), editing rhythm,
    typography, colour, layout, sound. <em>Conventions</em> are the settled habits of a genre —
    the newsreader behind a desk, the horror film's abandoned cabin, the magazine cover's
    masthead and coverline arrangement. The analytical move is denotation then connotation:
    first say what is literally there (low-angle shot, red masthead, slow-motion replay), then
    argue what it <em>means</em> (power, urgency, glory) — and support the connotation from the
    code itself. A low-angle shot and a 96-point headline are both sentences in their form's
    language.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example, a print advert: a new car photographed low, three-quarter on, on wet tarmac
    at dusk; the car is sharp, the city behind it blurred; the only text is the model name in
    condensed sans-serif and a one-word tagline. Denotation done. Connotations: the low angle
    grants the car monument status; the wet tarmac implies performance and control in
    conditions most drivers fear; the shallow focus isolates the product as the only fixed
    thing in a moving world; the near-absence of text says confidence — the product needs no
    persuasion, just naming. Genre codes (the "hero shot" inherited from decades of car
    advertising) let the reader decode it in two seconds. That two-second decoding is the point:
    media language works because audiences are fluent without knowing they are.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> media language is not only visual, and analysing it is not
  listing devices. A radio play's language is voice, silence, music and "soundscape"; a game's
  is mechanics and interface. And a list of codes earns nothing until each code is tied to the
  meaning it constructs — the mark schemes reward <em>analysis</em> (how the code works), not
  <em>identification</em> (that the code is present).</p>

  <h2>Representation: who is shown, and how</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Representation</strong> is how the media portray events, issues, individuals and
    social groups. Its founding insight — Stuart Hall's — is that representations are not
    windows onto the world but constructions of it: selecting, framing, repeating. The
    questions are structural. Who is shown, and who is absent? In what roles — hero, victim,
    comic relief, threat? Are individuals reduced to types? Stereotyping, in the classic
    definition, is representation by shorthand: a few repeated visual and verbal cues doing the
    work of a whole character, which is efficient for the producer and corrosive when the type
    carries a judgement — because repetition is how a media culture teaches its audiences who
    is "normal". Representation analysis also works at the level of issues: how does the news
    frame a protest (crime, chaos, or civic voice)? Which verbs attach to which group? Note the
    two-way street: media both reflect and shape attitudes, which is why a 1960s sitcom and a
    2020s one are different documents about the same society.
  </p>

  <h2>Media industries: who made it, and why</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Media industries</strong> analysis asks how the processes of production, distribution
    and circulation affect media forms and platforms — the honest answer to "why does this
    product exist in this shape?". Production: who funded it, and what did the funder want?
    Distribution: through what channels did it reach you — newsstand, cinema, broadcast
    schedule, platform algorithm? Circulation: how is it paid for — cover price, subscription,
    advertising, licence fee, in-game purchases? Each answer explains features of the product
    that media language alone cannot. A free-to-play mobile game is shaped, from its energy
    timers to its daily rewards, by the business model; a BBC drama is shaped by a public
    service remit that asks for something a streaming commission would not; a tabloid front
    page is shaped by the economics of the newsstand seconds in which a purchase is decided.
    Ownership matters structurally: when a few conglomerates own much of what a country reads
    and watches, the range of voices is an industrial question before it is an editorial one.
    Regulation — state and self-regulation, ratings, watershed, platform rules — is the
    industry's fourth wall, and comparing how different countries regulate the same content is
    a standard way to make the invisible hand visible.
  </p>

  <h2>Audiences: who it is for, and what they do with it</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>audiences</strong> area asks how media forms target, reach and address
    audiences; how audiences interpret and respond; and how members of audiences become
    producers themselves. "Target" is a design question: products are built for an imagined
    audience — a demographic profile, a psychographic one (attitudes, lifestyles) — and every
    code from media language is aimed at them. "Reach" is an industry question: schedulers,
    distributors and now recommendation algorithms decide who actually encounters the product.
    "Address" is a language question: the mode of address — direct to camera, the intimate
    second person of a podcast, the formal third person of a broadsheet — constructs a
    relationship. Interpretation is where the audience talks back: Hall's
    <strong>encoding/decoding</strong> model distinguishes the dominant reading the producer
    encodes, negotiated readings that accept some and resist other parts, and oppositional
    readings that understand and reject the message outright — the same text, three audiences.
  </p>
  <p>
    And "become producers themselves" is the newest part of the definition. The curve below is
    the number of people using social media worldwide — from under a billion in 2010 to more
    than five billion by 2024. It is the clearest single picture of why "audiences" now
    includes production: five billion people hold a channel to other audiences in their
    pockets, which is precisely how the syllabus phrases it — audience members "become
    producers themselves". It also reshaped the industries area, as distribution and
    advertising money followed the curve.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="global social media users, 2010–2024 — the audiences area rewriting itself in real time" widget="plotter" config={smCfg} />

  <p>
    Notice, too, what the curve implies about targeting — the first verb in the audiences
    area's own definition. An audience of five billion is really millions of overlapping
    micro-audiences: the same platforms that deliver a mass audience can address a
    sixteen-year-old skater in one city and a retiree in another with different feeds,
    advertising and even different news. When you analyse how a product "targets, reaches and
    addresses" its audience, you are being asked to name who it imagines watching, how it knows
    where to find them, and what tone it uses when it gets there — three questions that barely
    existed when audiences were measured by circulation figures and programme schedules.
  </p>

  <h2>The areas are inter-related: one product, four questions</h2>
  <p>
    The framework works because the areas are <em>inter-related</em>: the specification's word,
    and the difference between analysis and a checklist. Take one music video. Its
    <strong>media language</strong> — performance footage intercut with narrative, the star's
    wardrobe, the edit's pace — constructs meaning from codes you can name. Those codes carry
    its <strong>representations</strong>: of gender, wealth, place, who gets to be the hero.
    But the video exists because a <strong>media industry</strong> paid for it to promote a
    track, and the industry's platform — a streaming service's algorithm, a social feed —
    decides its reach. And its <strong>audience</strong> is not passive: viewers stream, skip,
    remix, comment and re-upload, which changes what the industry funds next. One product,
    four areas, all touching. That loop is the subject.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the four areas are not four separate topics to revise in
  isolation, and strong answers never do "the representation bit, then the industry bit".
  Examiners reward the connective moves: an industry constraint explaining a media-language
  choice, an audience practice (memes, reaction videos) feeding back into representation. If
  your analysis could be cut into four disconnected paragraphs, it has not yet used the
  framework.</p>

  <h2>Reading through contexts</h2>
  <p>
    Alongside the framework, the specification requires three <strong>contexts</strong> — the
    circumstances a product is made in and reflects. <strong>Historical context</strong>: how
    the product shows the time it was made, in its language, genre conventions, representations
    and in how it was produced, distributed and consumed (a magazine sold at a newsstand
    belongs to a different media world from one distributed as an app). <strong>Social and
    cultural context</strong>: how the product reflects the society around it, the genres,
    styles and technologies it borrows — and how audience responses to it change over time, so
    a representation that read as harmless in one decade can read as damaging in the next.
    <strong>Political context</strong>: the politics inside a product's viewpoints and
    representations, and the politics around it — ownership, regulation, censorship. The habit
    to build is asking of any product: <em>when</em> is this from, <em>what society</em> is it
    speaking to, and <em>whose interests</em> does it serve?
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Underneath the four areas sit the subject's theoretical traditions, worth knowing as
    Toolkit versions of the framework. <em>Semiotics</em> (Saussure, Barthes) formalises media
    language: the sign, the denoted and the connoted, and Barthes' <em>myth</em> — the moment a
    cultural meaning presents itself as natural fact. <em>Reception theory</em> (Hall's
    encoding/decoding, and the active-audience tradition that follows) formalises the
    audiences area's interpretive half. <em>Gender and representation theory</em> (from
    Mulvey's "male gaze" to contemporary debates about who authors images of whom) formalises
    the representation area. <em>Political economy</em> — ownership, funding, concentration —
    formalises the industries area; <em>regulation theory</em> tracks how states and platforms
    govern content. The framework's four areas are the exam's map; these traditions are the
    surveying instruments beneath it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open questions the subject is currently living through are worth stating, because they
    are the areas in motion at once. Measurement: audiences are now both counted and
    <em>constructed</em> by platforms, which sell "engagement" metrics the advertisers then buy
    — a loop in which the industry's own instruments shape the audience they claim merely to
    observe. Participation: the prosumer (producer-consumer) disrupts the industries area's old
    categories — is a viral fan edit an audience response or an unlicensed production? — and
    regulation has not settled the answer. And algorithmic curation changes media language
    itself: formats evolve to win the first two seconds of a feed, which is a real, measurable
    industrial pressure on form. The framework was designed for newspapers and broadcasts; its
    passing test is whether four questions asked honestly still decode a product that is
    distributed by a recommendation engine — and every indication so far is that they do,
    provided you let them talk to each other.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WJEC Eduqas GCSE Media Studies specification, "Theoretical framework" and "Contexts of media" (components 1–2); DataReportal / We Are Social, <em>Digital</em> global reports (2010–2024); S. Hall, "Encoding/Decoding in the Television Discourse" (1973/1980); R. Barthes, <em>Mythologies</em> (1957); L. Mulvey, "Visual Pleasure and Narrative Cinema" (1975).</p>
